 ## U9 dependency introduction (2026-10-17)
 
 The official tarball is now accessible (HTTP 200, 2,409,319 bytes). Its SHA-512 exactly matches U1's committed qualification lockfile: `sha512-oLDq3jw7AcLqKWH2AhCpVTZl8mf6X2YReP+Neh0SJUzV/BdZYjth94tG5toiMB1PPrYtxOCfaoUCkvtuH+3AJA==`. The unchanged archive is vendored at `vendor/xlsx-0.20.3.tgz`, installed through a local file reference with the same lockfile integrity, and bundled in the processing worker. Apache-2.0 is retained in the tarball, `vendor/SheetJS-LICENSE.txt`, and the distributed static `SheetJS-LICENSE.txt` asset.
 
 Manual advisory recheck retrieved GHSA-4r6h-8v6p-xvw6 and GHSA-5pgg-2g8v-p4x9 on 2026-10-17; their affected ranges remain before 0.19.3 and before 0.20.2 respectively. The pages' public-npm patched-version field says “None”; this does not identify the current official CDN release as affected. `npm audit` reports zero known vulnerabilities for the introduced tree; this is supplemental evidence, not a safety guarantee.
 
 U9 uses the preflight implementation and review evidence preserved at commit `81f74a2` unchanged. Every discovery/import first validates the complete original archive. Multiple sheets require selection; saved workflows reuse the explicit worksheet name and reject a missing sheet. Templates have independent sheet selection and import names/order only. The explicit “Excel date cell” setting interprets date-formatted numeric cells with their workbook calendar, rejecting serial 60/zero in 1900, fractions, negative and out-of-calendar values. Raw metadata remains inspectable; formulas are never evaluated and referenced usable caches carry stale warnings. Missing/error caches invalidate dependent output, including optional fields. Only actual formula-like output text requires the CSV escape/raw decision.
 
 See `docs/reviews/u9/verification.md` for integrated-worker verification, screenshots, exact environment and remaining acceptance limits. XLSX output remains U10; reference-laptop and final release qualification remain U13.
+
+## U10 value-only writer (2026-10-18)
+
+The application now constructs a fresh SheetJS workbook from evaluated `Value` objects only, with one `Output` worksheet and explicit shared-string or numeric cells. No source workbook/cell objects enter the writer. Column order, header choice and blank table extent are explicit. Dates use integer Gregorian calendar arithmetic, a deliberate 1900/1904 epoch and one of the three approved number formats; dates before that epoch fail with CSV/text alternatives. No JavaScript `Date` or timezone conversion participates.
+
+Numeric output is conservatively qualified at no more than 15 significant decimal digits, within Excel's normal range, with exact Big.js equality against the serialized JavaScript number's decimal representation. Overflow, underflow, subnormal values and precision changes block export with row/field/action context. Numeric lexical formatting is not an identifier contract; explicit text output preserves such spellings.
+
+Integrated tests identified a SheetJS string boundary: its writer does not protect literal `_xHHHH_` tokens, and its `t="str"` reader decodes escapes twice. The adapter protects each initial underscore using OOXML `_x005F_` encoding (including overlapping tokens) and writes shared strings, which the reader decodes once. Independent ZIP/XML checks verify the actual representation; rereading alone would hide CRLF normalization performed by the library. Text length and invalid Unicode are checked before writing, including the writer's length limit after token protection. The vendored dependency remains unchanged.
+
+An additional scratch probe and failing regression demonstrated that `book_new()` alone is not an isolation boundary: SheetJS's global SSF table retains imported custom number formats, and its default writer copies them into a fresh workbook. The adapter explicitly supplies an independent SSF table containing only General and the three approved date formats at custom IDs 164–166. A source-only private format marker is absent from every generated ZIP member; browser inspection also rejects the source fixture's unused `[DBNum1]` format. No source format metadata or library-global custom format survives this boundary.
+
+Output amplification is bounded at 2 million table positions, Excel's row/text limits and a conservative 100 MiB serialization estimate (128 bytes per position, seven times UTF-8 text bytes and 64 KiB package reserve). This can reject a highly repetitive/escape-heavy workbook below 100 MiB actual expansion; it is intentionally an allocation safeguard, with fewer-column/smaller-file remediation and no truncation. The original 25 MiB input, actual-expansion/CRC/archive checks, worker cancellation and 30-second watchdog remain in place. U10 repeats hostile archives through the integrated worker and measures 10,000 × 20 CSV/XLSX import-through-Excel-download completeness and responsiveness. Exact test results, browser availability and release qualification limits are recorded in `docs/reviews/u10/verification.md`; full reference-device qualification remains U13.
